<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="mevalonate pathway I (eukaryotes and bacteria)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-922" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The biosynthesis of |FRAME: DELTA3-ISOPENTENYL-PP| (isopentenyl pyrophosphate, IPP) can occur via two distinct routes: the |FRAME: PWY-922 &quot;mevalonate pathway&quot;| (MVA pathway, such as this pathway) and the |FRAME:NONMEVIPP-PWY &quot;methylerythritol phosphate pathway&quot;| (MEP pathway). In the former, IPP is synthesized from the condensation of three acetyl-CoA molecules; in contrast, in the MEP pathway IPP is synthesized via the condensation of pyruvate and D-glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate. For many years, the MVA pathway was considered to be the sole source of IPP in all living organisms, until several inconsistencies led to the discovery of the MEP pathway in bacteria and plants. The MEP pathway is present in most eubacteria, in unicellular green algae, in the chloroplasts of phototrophic organisms and in some unicellular eukaryotes related to photosynthetic phyla, such as the Plasmodium parasites. The mevalonate pathway is found in animals, fungi, the cytoplasm of phototrophic organisms, archaea, and some eubacteria. A considerable variability exists among archaea, and at least four different variants of the MVA pathway have been described from that kingdom. The |FRAME: PWY-922 &quot;eukaryotic variant&quot;| is rare in archaea, but occurs in members of the order |FRAME: TAX-2281|, such as |FRAME: TAX-2287| |CITS: [23378249]|. While most archaea posses a variant now known as the |FRAME: PWY-8125 &quot;archaeal pathway&quot;| |CITS: [30224495]|, a |FRAME: PWY-6174 &quot;third variant&quot;| has been described from halophilic archaea such as |FRAME: TAX-2246| |CITS: [24375100]| (that pathway was also found in the eubacterium |FRAME: TAX-120962| |CITS: [24327557]|), and a |FRAME: PWY-7524 &quot;fourth variant&quot;| has been described from archaea of the order |FRAME: TAX-2301| |CITS: [24755225][25636853]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; In eukaryotic cells, the mevalonate pathway leads to |FRAME:PWY-2541|, |FRAME: PWY-6075| and dolichol biosynthesis via the formation of farnesyl diphosphate (FPP). IPP from this pathway is also used to synthesize cytosolic |FRAME: GERANYLGERANYL-PP| (GGPP), which is used (along with FPP) for the prenylation of proteins. In plants, the mevalonate pathway is also a source of isoprene units for the biosynthesis of a variety of terpenoids (e.g. cytokinins, brassinosteroids, sesquiterpenes, polyprenoids). The enzymes of this pathway have all been isolated and characterized from bacteria. Additionally, a number of plant homologues have been demonstrated to be fully functional, largely through functional complementation of bacterial and yeast mutant strains.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="O-18" />
    <Author name="pellegrini-toole" />
  </Authors>
